#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075034 :tutorial/content "We can find **equivalent ratios** by multiplying or dividing all numbers in a ratio by the same value.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following ratio:\n\n$$\n2:6\n$$\n\nIf we multiply *both* numbers by $5$, for example, we get the ratio $10:30$.\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}: & 6 \\\\ ↓ \\cdot 5 & ↓ \\cdot 5 \\\\ 10 &:\\end{vmatrix}30\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios $2:6$ and $10:30$ are equivalent.\n\nSimilarly, if we take the ratio $2:6$ and divide *both* numbers by $2$, we get $1:3$.\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}: & 6 \\\\ ↓ \\div 2 & ↓ \\div 2 \\\\ 1 &:\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios $2:6$ and $1:3$ are also equivalent." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc2c9ce9-c2b0-5d68-81db-b8b0273407f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8203 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075035 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the following two ratios are equivalent:\n\n$$\n0\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing number?\n\nTo find a ratio equivalent to $3: 5$, we multiply or divide both numbers by the same value.\n\nIn this case, we need to get $12$ from ${\\color{blue}{3}}$, so we multiply ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ by $4$.\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}: & 5 \\\\ ↓ \\cdot 4 & ↓ \\cdot 4 \\\\ 12 &:\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\phantom{0}[/math]\n$$\n\nSo, to get $0$ from ${\\color{red}{5}}$, we multiply ${\\color{red}{5}}$ by $4$ as well:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\times 4 = \\bbox[3px,lightgray]{\\color{red}20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $20$." :tutorial/id #uuid "3539d419-9d5a-5467-8a88-7ef318329859" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8204 :tutorial/title "Finding Missing Values in Equivalent Ratios"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075036 :tutorial/content "We can simplify ratios by dividing both numbers in the ratio by a common factor.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the following ratio:\n\n$$\n18:9\n$$\n\nThe numbers $18$ and $9$ share a common factor of $9$, so we can divide both numbers by $9$ as follows:\n\n$$\n18\\begin{vmatrix}: & 9 \\\\ ↓ \\div 9 & ↓ \\div 9 \\\\ 2 &:\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18:9$ is equivalent to $2:1$.\n\nSince $2$ and $1$ have no common factors, $2:1$ is the simplest form of this ratio. We often prefer to work with ratios in their simplest form, where possible." :tutorial/id #uuid "c7ece136-e063-594d-b25d-4cf19bca7890" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8205 :tutorial/title "Expressing Ratios in Their Simplest Form"}]}
